/**
 * @defgroup dryer_operating_cost_calculator Dryer Operating Cost Calculator
 * @ingroup compressed_air_calculators
 * @brief Calculates moisture removal and annual operating cost for compressed-air dryers.
 * @details The calculator supports Heatless, Externally Heated, Blower Purge with
 * and without Sweep, Heat of Compression HC and SP, and Refrigerated dryers.
 * Input validation, type defaults, and user-facing unit conversion belong to the
 * caller. The Suite receives U.S. customary engineering inputs and returns water
 * removal in lb/hr and annual cost in dollars/year.
 *
 * @heading{Public API}
 * The C++ implementation follows the stateless compressed-air calculator pattern:
 * callers populate @ref dryer_operating_cost::Input, including the dryer type, and
 * pass it to @ref dryer_operating_cost::calculate. The WebAssembly binding exposes
 * the equivalent `calculateDryerOperatingCost(input)` function. Both the JavaScript
 * input and result are plain value objects and require no `delete()` cleanup.
 *
 * @heading{Water load}
 * Saturation pressure is calculated by @ref SaturatedPressure at the inlet
 * temperature. Refrigerated dryers subtract saturation pressure at 40 degrees F.
 *
 * @formula{dryer-water-load;
 *   W = \frac{Q \cdot 60 \cdot 18.016 \cdot (P_{sat,in} - P_{sat,out})}
 *            {(P_g + 14.7) \cdot 379}
 * }
 *
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{W; Water removed; lb/hr}
 * @symrow{Q; Inlet airflow; SCFM}
 * @symrow{P_{sat,in}; Saturation pressure at inlet temperature; psia}
 * @symrow{P_{sat,out}; Zero for desiccant dryers or saturation pressure at 40 degrees F for refrigerated dryers; psia}
 * @symrow{P_g; Inlet gauge pressure; psig}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @heading{Desiccant and dryer sizing}
 * Heatless dryers preserve the source calculator's distinct sizing relation:
 * @formula{dryer-heatless-size;
 *   V = Q \cdot \frac{114.7}{P_g + 14.7}
 * }
 * Other desiccant dryers use:
 * @formula{dryer-desiccant-size;
 *   m_d = \frac{W \cdot t_c}{f_{DDC}}, \qquad V = \frac{m_d}{0.5784}
 * }
 * where @math{t_c} is regeneration half-cycle length in hours and
 * @math{f_{DDC}} is the design desiccant capacity percentage divided by 100.
 * SP dryers use the caller-supplied v2 values of two hours and four percent.
 *
 * @heading{Purge, heater, and motor}
 * Purge airflow is either @math{V \cdot f_p} or a direct SCFM input. Direct flow
 * is converted to an effective capacity percentage for the output. Automatic
 * heater and motor formulas follow the Dryer Calculator V2 formula reference;
 * positive heater kW or motor HP inputs override automatic sizing where the
 * selected dryer type permits manual sizing. HC heater power is always calculated
 * from inlet flow.
 *
 * @heading{Annual cost}
 * @formula{dryer-total-cost;
 *   C_{total} = C_{heater} + C_{motor} + C_{purge} + C_{controls} + C_{cooling}
 * }
 * Heater energy is scaled by heating hours per day and annual operating hours
 * divided by 24. Motors run for all annual operating hours. Purge cost uses the
 * greater of the entered compressed-air rate and its electricity-equivalent rate,
 * multiplied by the dryer-type duty factor. Desiccant dryers include a 100 W
 * controls load; only refrigerated dryers include cooling-water cost.
 *
 * @heading{Migration from v1}
 * Replace construction of `DryerOperatingCost` and its instance `calculate` call
 * with one `calculateDryerOperatingCost` call. Move `dryerType` into the input
 * object and remove cleanup calls for the calculator and returned result. The v2
 * input also replaces the three schedule fields with annual operating hours and
 * adds regeneration-cycle, purge-mode, direct-purge-flow, and motor-power fields.
 *
 */
